.TH CDYNMQ_TRY_RECV 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cdynmq_try_recv \- try to receive from a dynamic queue with no block
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t cdynmq_try_recv(cdynmq *dq,
                              cmessage_t *target_buf);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Removes the message at the head of the dynamic queue and gives it back
in target_buf. When the queue is empty the call returns
ccol_container_empty at once; it never waits for a message.

On success the message's data pointer belongs to the caller:
target_buf->data is the pointer the sender gave and target_buf->size is
its size in bytes, and the caller frees the data when done with it. No
bytes are copied, only the pointer and its size move. A sender can put
a message with a NULL data pointer and a size of zero into the queue,
and it arrives the same way, so target_buf->data can legitimately be
NULL with target_buf->size at 0. Check it before you dereference it.

On any failure target_buf is left unchanged, and the queue keeps
everything it held.

Use this receive call immediately after a callback from cselect(),
cselect_timed(), or an ceventloop reports that a dynamic queue
selectable is ready. Those report readiness only and never take the
message themselves, so another consumer can take the message first;
ccol_container_empty covers exactly that case.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
dq          Dynamic queue to receive from
target_buf  Buffer that gets the message together with the ownership
            of the data.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success         - A message was received. The caller owns
                       target_buf->data and must free it.
ccol_invalid_args    - dq or target_buf is NULL
ccol_container_empty - The queue is empty; target_buf is left
                       unchanged.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
This call never blocks. It works while sends on the queue are off,
because turning sends off only keeps new messages out, and a receive
still gets the ones the queue already holds. A successful receive wakes
one write-direction waiter on the queue (a cselect() call, a
cselect_timed() call or a ceventloop registration). The
function is thread-safe.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cdynmq_recv (3),
.BR cdynmq_timed_recv (3),
.BR cdynmq_send (3),
.BR cdynmq_msg_count (3),
.BR cselect (3)
